%I A398900 #35 Sep 17 2026 17:40:06
%S A398900 2,3,5,7,11,23,29,71
%N A398900 Prime numbers that can be written as half the sum of three squares in exactly one unique way.
%C A398900 There are only eight terms and no prime p > 71 has a unique solution due to class number growth restrictions under the Stark-Heegner theorem.
%C A398900 The sum of the given primes is 151 (prime) and the sum of the primes missing from the list (up to 71) is 488. So we have 151 + 488 = 639 = 9 * 71.
%C A398900 The difference between the sums is 337 (also prime). Also, twice the sum of this set plus 337 equals 639.
%C A398900 The sum of the missing primes (488) factors 8 * 61. While the the sum of all the primes up to the last in this series (639) factors 9 * 71.
%F A398900 { prime p : 2*p in { A094942 } }.
%e A398900 2: (2^2 + 0^2 + 0^2) / 2 = (4 + 0 + 0) / 2 = 2
%e A398900 3: (2^2 + 1^2 + 1^2) / 2 = (4 + 1 + 1) / 2 = 3
%e A398900 5: (3^2 + 1^2 + 0^2) / 2 = (9 + 1 + 0) / 2 = 5
%e A398900 7: (3^2 + 2^2 + 1^2) / 2 = (9 + 4 + 1) / 2 = 7
%e A398900 11: (3^2 + 3^2 + 2^2) / 2 = (9 + 9 + 4) / 2 = 11
%e A398900 23: (6^2 + 3^2 + 1^2) / 2 = (36 + 9 + 1) / 2 = 23
%e A398900 29: (7^2 + 3^2 + 0^2) / 2 = (49 + 9 + 0) / 2 = 29
%e A398900 71: (9^2 + 6^2 + 5^2) / 2 = (81 + 36 + 25) / 2 = 71
%Y A398900 Cf. A000040, A003173, A094942.
%K A398900 nonn,fini,full,new
%O A398900 1,1
%A A398900 _Martin Michael Musatov_, Sep 11 2026
